## The three forms

```jsx
useEffect(() => { … });              // after EVERY render
useEffect(() => { … }, []);           // once, after mount
useEffect(() => { … }, [userId]);     // when userId changes
```

---

## Cleanup

The returned function runs before the effect re-runs, and on unmount:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const connection = createConnection(roomId);
  connection.connect();

  return () => connection.disconnect();   // ← before the next run, and on unmount
}, [roomId]);
```

Change `roomId` from A to B and the order is: disconnect from A, then connect to B. Setup and teardown live next to each other, which is the main ergonomic win over class lifecycles.

---

## The mental model that helps most

Stop thinking "on mount / on update". Think **synchronisation**:

> This effect keeps something outside React in sync with these values. Whenever they change, re-synchronise.

From that angle the dependency array is not a configuration option — it is a description of what the effect reads. That is why the lint rule can compute it for you.

---

## Comparison is by reference

```jsx
// ❌ A new object every render → the effect runs every render
useEffect(() => { … }, [{ id: userId }]);

// ❌ Same problem with an inline function
useEffect(() => { … }, [() => fetchUser(userId)]);

// ✅ Depend on primitives
useEffect(() => { … }, [userId]);

// ✅ Or stabilise the object
const options = useMemo(() => ({ id: userId }), [userId]);
useEffect(() => { … }, [options]);
```

---

## Do not lie to the linter

```jsx
// ❌ Suppressing the warning creates a stale closure
useEffect(() => {
  doSomething(count);
  // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
}, []);
```

If a dependency causes the effect to run too often, the effect is structured wrong. Options that keep it honest: use the functional state updater so you do not need the value, move the value inside the effect, or extract the changing function outside the component.

---

## Cleaning up async work

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();

  fetch(`/api/users/${id}`, { signal: controller.signal })
    .then((r) => r.json())
    .then(setUser)
    .catch((e) => { if (e.name !== 'AbortError') setError(e); });

  return () => controller.abort();   // stops a stale response overwriting fresh data
}, [id]);
```

Without the abort, switching quickly from user 1 to user 2 can leave user 1's slower response arriving last and winning. This race is the single most common effect bug.

## The mapping

| Class | Hook |
|-------|------|
| `constructor` | `useState(initial)` |
| `componentDidMount` | `useEffect(fn, [])` |
| `componentDidUpdate` | `useEffect(fn, [deps])` |
| `componentWillUnmount` | `return () => {}` inside an effect |
| `getDerivedStateFromProps` | Compute during render, or use `key` |
| `shouldComponentUpdate` | `memo` |
| `getSnapshotBeforeUpdate` | `useLayoutEffect` |
| `componentDidCatch` | **No hook** — still needs a class |

---

## All three, in one effect

```jsx
// Class — one concern, three methods
class Chat extends React.Component {
  componentDidMount() {
    this.conn = connect(this.props.roomId);
  }
  componentDidUpdate(prevProps) {
    if (prevProps.roomId !== this.props.roomId) {
      this.conn.close();
      this.conn = connect(this.props.roomId);
    }
  }
  componentWillUnmount() {
    this.conn.close();
  }
}

// Hook — one concern, one block
function Chat({ roomId }) {
  useEffect(() => {
    const conn = connect(roomId);
    return () => conn.close();
  }, [roomId]);
}
```

The class version has the close logic in two places, and forgetting the `prevProps` comparison is a classic bug. The hook version cannot have that bug, because React handles the transition.

---

## Where the mapping is misleading

**`useEffect(fn, [])` is not exactly `componentDidMount`.** It runs after paint, whereas `componentDidMount` runs before it. And under StrictMode in development it runs twice.

**`useEffect` with deps is not exactly `componentDidUpdate`.** It also runs on mount. If you truly need update-only behaviour:

```jsx
const isFirst = useRef(true);
useEffect(() => {
  if (isFirst.current) { isFirst.current = false; return; }
  // update-only logic
}, [value]);
```

Needing this is usually a sign the logic belongs in an event handler instead.

---

## Stop translating

The best advice for anyone moving from classes: **do not port lifecycle by lifecycle.** Ask "what external system is this synchronising with, and what does it depend on?" and write one effect per system. You typically end up with fewer effects than you had lifecycle branches.

## Anti-pattern 1 — transforming data

```jsx
// ❌ Extra render, and the value is briefly wrong
const [total, setTotal] = useState(0);
useEffect(() => {
  setTotal(items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.price, 0));
}, [items]);

// ✅ Just calculate it
const total = items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.price, 0);
```

---

## Anti-pattern 2 — handling an event

```jsx
// ❌ Now every path that sets `submitted` sends an analytics event
useEffect(() => {
  if (submitted) analytics.track('purchase');
}, [submitted]);

// ✅ The event caused it, so put it in the handler
function handleSubmit() {
  analytics.track('purchase');
  setSubmitted(true);
}
```

The effect version also fires again if `submitted` is reset and set once more.

---

## Anti-pattern 3 — resetting state on a prop change

```jsx
// ❌ Renders once with the wrong state, then corrects itself
useEffect(() => {
  setComment('');
}, [postId]);

// ✅ A new key gives you a genuinely fresh component
<CommentBox key={postId} postId={postId} />
```

---

## Anti-pattern 4 — chained effects

```jsx
// ❌ Three renders, and the logic is scattered
useEffect(() => { if (card) setGoldCount(c => c + 1); }, [card]);
useEffect(() => { if (goldCount > 3) setRound(r => r + 1); }, [goldCount]);
useEffect(() => { if (round > 5) setGameOver(true); }, [round]);

// ✅ One handler, all the logic visible in one place
function handleCardPlayed(card) {
  const nextGold = goldCount + 1;
  setGoldCount(nextGold);
  if (nextGold > 3) { … }
}
```

---

## What effects ARE for

| Legitimate | Example |
|------------|---------|
| Subscriptions | WebSocket, event emitter, store |
| Browser APIs | `addEventListener`, `IntersectionObserver` |
| Timers | `setInterval` with cleanup |
| Non-React libraries | Chart, map, editor instances |
| Fetching (if you have no data library) | With `AbortController` cleanup |
| Syncing to storage | `localStorage`, `document.title` |

---

## The test

> Is there a system **outside React** that needs to stay in sync?

No → do not use an effect. Yes → use one, and clean it up.

> The React docs have a whole page titled *You Might Not Need an Effect*. That title is the answer to this question.

## The naive version and what breaks

```jsx
// ❌ Race condition: switch from user 1 to user 2 quickly and
// user 1's slower response can arrive last and win.
useEffect(() => {
  fetch(`/api/users/${id}`).then(r => r.json()).then(setUser);
}, [id]);
```

---

## Doing it correctly by hand

```jsx
function useUser(id) {
  const [state, setState] = useState({ status: 'loading', data: null, error: null });

  useEffect(() => {
    const controller = new AbortController();
    setState({ status: 'loading', data: null, error: null });

    fetch(`/api/users/${id}`, { signal: controller.signal })
      .then((r) => {
        if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${r.status}`);   // fetch does NOT throw on 404/500
        return r.json();
      })
      .then((data) => setState({ status: 'success', data, error: null }))
      .catch((error) => {
        if (error.name === 'AbortError') return;          // expected on cleanup
        setState({ status: 'error', data: null, error });
      });

    return () => controller.abort();
  }, [id]);

  return state;
}
```

That is the *minimum* correct version, and it still has no cache, no deduplication, no retry and no revalidation on window focus.

---

## What you are missing

| Concern | Hand-rolled | React Query / SWR |
|---------|-------------|-------------------|
| Race conditions | You handle it | Built in |
| Caching | None | By query key |
| Deduplication | Two components = two requests | One request, shared |
| Refetch on focus/reconnect | No | Yes |
| Retry with backoff | No | Yes |
| Pagination, infinite scroll | Manual | Built in |

```jsx
import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';

function Profile({ id }) {
  const { data, isPending, error } = useQuery({
    queryKey: ['user', id],
    queryFn: ({ signal }) => fetch(`/api/users/${id}`, { signal }).then(r => r.json()),
  });

  if (isPending) return <Spinner />;
  if (error) return <Error error={error} />;
  return <h1>{data.name}</h1>;
}
```

---

## The framing that impresses

**Server state is not client state.** It is cached, it can go stale, it is shared, and it can fail. Storing it in `useState` means reimplementing a cache badly. That is the argument for a data library, and it is a better answer than "React Query is nicer".

---

## The modern option

```jsx
// Server Component — runs on the server, no effect, no loading state
async function Profile({ id }) {
  const user = await db.user.findUnique({ where: { id } });
  return <h1>{user.name}</h1>;
}
```

No race condition, because there is no re-render to race with.

## What React is testing

A user navigates to a page, leaves, and comes back. Your effect must survive that. StrictMode compresses it into a single mount so you find out immediately.

```text
StrictMode (dev):  mount → cleanup → mount
Production:        mount
```

---

## What it catches

```jsx
// ❌ Every mount adds a listener; none are removed → leak
useEffect(() => {
  window.addEventListener('resize', onResize);
}, []);

// Under StrictMode you now have two listeners. In production,
// a user who visits the page five times has five.

// ✅
useEffect(() => {
  window.addEventListener('resize', onResize);
  return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', onResize);
}, []);
```

Same story with intervals, subscriptions and animation frames — the double run makes an existing leak visible.

---

## The wrong fix

```jsx
// ❌ Silences the warning, keeps the bug
const ran = useRef(false);
useEffect(() => {
  if (ran.current) return;
  ran.current = true;
  fetchData();
}, []);
```

This says "never run twice". But in production the effect *will* run again on a genuine remount, and now it will not — because the ref only resets when the component is truly destroyed. You have traded a duplicate request for a page that sometimes shows no data.

---

## The right fix for fetching

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal }).then(…);
  return () => controller.abort();
}, [url]);
```

The first request is aborted by the cleanup. Two requests are fired; one is cancelled. Harmless, and correct on real remounts too.

Or use React Query, which deduplicates identical in-flight requests automatically — the double mount produces exactly one network call.

---

## What if the effect genuinely cannot run twice?

Something like "charge the customer" should not be in an effect at all. It belongs in an event handler, where it runs once per click by definition. If an effect is not safe to run twice, that is usually the real signal.

---

## In summary

> Development only. Never happens in production. Removing `<StrictMode>` to stop it is exchanging a visible warning for an invisible leak.

## The pattern

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  // setup
  const thing = createThing();

  // teardown — runs before re-run and on unmount
  return () => thing.destroy();
}, [deps]);
```

---

## The common cases

```jsx
// Event listener
useEffect(() => {
  const onKey = (e) => e.key === 'Escape' && close();
  document.addEventListener('keydown', onKey);
  return () => document.removeEventListener('keydown', onKey);
}, [close]);

// Interval
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(tick, 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);

// Observer
useEffect(() => {
  const observer = new IntersectionObserver(onIntersect);
  observer.observe(ref.current);
  return () => observer.disconnect();
}, []);

// WebSocket
useEffect(() => {
  const socket = new WebSocket(url);
  socket.onmessage = handleMessage;
  return () => socket.close();
}, [url]);

// Third-party library
useEffect(() => {
  const chart = new Chart(canvasRef.current, config);
  return () => chart.destroy();
}, [config]);
```

---

## The listener mistake

```jsx
// ❌ Two different function objects — remove does nothing
useEffect(() => {
  window.addEventListener('resize', () => setWidth(window.innerWidth));
  return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', () => setWidth(window.innerWidth));
}, []);
```

`removeEventListener` matches by reference. Two identical-looking arrow functions are different objects, so the listener stays attached forever. Always name the handler.

---

## Cancelling async work

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal }).then(setData);
  return () => controller.abort();
}, [url]);
```

For a promise you cannot abort, use a flag:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  let cancelled = false;
  loadThing().then((d) => { if (!cancelled) setData(d); });
  return () => { cancelled = true; };
}, []);
```

---

## How to spot a leak

Open DevTools, navigate between pages repeatedly, and watch the heap. A memory graph that only climbs means something is not being cleaned up. StrictMode's double mount surfaces many of these immediately in development — which is exactly what it is for.

## Why it matters

Since React 16, an uncaught render error **unmounts the entire tree**. One broken component turns the whole page blank. An error boundary contains the damage.

---

## The implementation

```jsx
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  state = { error: null };

  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {
    return { error };            // render phase — update state to show the fallback
  }

  componentDidCatch(error, info) {
    logToService(error, info.componentStack);   // commit phase — side effects go here
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.error) {
      return this.props.fallback ?? <p>Something went wrong.</p>;
    }
    return this.props.children;
  }
}
```

There is still **no hook equivalent**, which is the main reason classes are not fully gone. In practice most teams use `react-error-boundary`, which wraps this and adds a `resetErrorBoundary` function.

---

## What it does not catch

| Not caught | Why | What to do |
|------------|-----|------------|
| Event handlers | Not part of rendering | `try/catch` in the handler |
| `setTimeout`, promises | Runs outside the render stack | `.catch()` or `try/catch` |
| Server-side rendering | Boundaries are client-side | Handle it on the server |
| Errors in the boundary itself | It cannot catch its own | Nest another boundary above |

```jsx
// ❌ The boundary will not see this
<button onClick={() => { throw new Error('boom'); }} />

// ✅ Route it into React by putting the error in state
function Component() {
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);
  if (error) throw error;                    // now it throws during render

  return <button onClick={() => {
    try { risky(); } catch (e) { setError(e); }
  }} />;
}
```

---

## Where to put them

Not one at the root. Place them at meaningful boundaries so a failure degrades gracefully:

```jsx
<ErrorBoundary fallback={<AppCrashed />}>       {/* last resort */}
  <Layout>
    <ErrorBoundary fallback={<WidgetFailed />}>  {/* one widget dies alone */}
      <RevenueChart />
    </ErrorBoundary>
    <ErrorBoundary fallback={<FeedFailed />}>
      <ActivityFeed />
    </ErrorBoundary>
  </Layout>
</ErrorBoundary>
```

A broken chart should not take down the navigation.

---

## Pair it with Suspense

```jsx
<ErrorBoundary fallback={<Error />}>
  <Suspense fallback={<Spinner />}>
    <Profile />
  </Suspense>
</ErrorBoundary>
```

Suspense handles "still loading", the boundary handles "it failed". Together they cover both async outcomes.

## The mechanism

```jsx
function useUpdateEffect(fn, deps) {
  const isFirst = useRef(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (isFirst.current) {
      isFirst.current = false;
      return;
    }
    return fn();
  }, deps);
}

// Usage
useUpdateEffect(() => {
  showToast('Filters updated');
}, [filters]);
```

---

## The StrictMode wrinkle

Under StrictMode the component mounts, unmounts and mounts again. The ref keeps its value across that sequence for the same component instance, so the second mount is treated as an "update" and your effect fires when you did not expect it.

A more robust version resets in cleanup:

```jsx
function useUpdateEffect(fn, deps) {
  const isFirst = useRef(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    isFirst.current = true;
    return () => { isFirst.current = true; };
  }, []);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (isFirst.current) { isFirst.current = false; return; }
    return fn();
  }, deps);
}
```

The fact that this needs careful handling is itself a hint that the pattern is fighting the framework.

---

## Why you usually should not need it

Most "skip the mount" cases are event logic in disguise:

```jsx
// ❌ Effect + skip-first-render dance
useUpdateEffect(() => {
  analytics.track('filter_changed', filters);
}, [filters]);

// ✅ The user changed the filter. Track it there.
function handleFilterChange(next) {
  setFilters(next);
  analytics.track('filter_changed', next);
}
```

The handler version is also more correct: it fires once per user action, not once per state change from any source.

---

## The legitimate cases

They exist, but they are narrow:

- Animating a value *transition* — there is no previous value on mount
- Persisting a change without overwriting stored data with the initial value on first load
- Showing a "saved" indicator that should not appear before anything is saved

---

## A cleaner alternative

Compare against the previous value explicitly. It expresses the intent directly instead of relying on render counting:

```jsx
const prevFilters = usePrevious(filters);

useEffect(() => {
  if (prevFilters === undefined) return;    // mount
  if (prevFilters === filters) return;      // no real change
  onFiltersChanged(filters);
}, [filters, prevFilters]);
```

## The basic use — code splitting

```jsx
import { lazy, Suspense } from 'react';

const Dashboard = lazy(() => import('./Dashboard'));

<Suspense fallback={<Skeleton />}>
  <Dashboard />
</Suspense>
```

The bundle for `Dashboard` is only downloaded when it first renders. Until then, the fallback shows.

---

## What it replaces

```jsx
// ❌ Loading state threaded through every component
function Page() {
  const { data, isLoading } = useUser();
  if (isLoading) return <Spinner />;
  return <Profile user={data} />;
}

// ✅ The boundary owns the loading UI
<Suspense fallback={<Spinner />}>
  <Profile />          {/* just reads data; never sees a loading flag */}
</Suspense>
```

The component becomes simpler because it only handles the success case. Loading moves up to a boundary and error handling moves to an error boundary.

---

## Data fetching with Suspense

This requires a **framework or a supporting library** — you cannot make a plain `fetch` suspend:

```jsx
// React 19 + a framework
function Comments({ commentsPromise }) {
  const comments = use(commentsPromise);   // suspends until resolved
  return <ul>{comments.map(c => <li key={c.id}>{c.text}</li>)}</ul>;
}
```

React Query supports it via `useSuspenseQuery`. Next.js App Router does it natively with async Server Components.

---

## Boundary placement is a design decision

```jsx
// One boundary — the whole page waits for the slowest part
<Suspense fallback={<PageSkeleton />}>
  <Header /><Feed /><Sidebar />
</Suspense>

// Separate boundaries — each section appears as soon as it is ready
<Header />
<Suspense fallback={<FeedSkeleton />}><Feed /></Suspense>
<Suspense fallback={<SidebarSkeleton />}><Sidebar /></Suspense>
```

The second version streams: the server sends the shell immediately and fills in each section as its data resolves.

---

## What Suspense does not do

- It does not fetch anything — it only reacts to something signalling it is not ready
- It does not catch errors — pair it with an error boundary
- It has no timeout or retry — the library or framework owns that

---

## Avoiding fallback flashes

```jsx
const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();

startTransition(() => setTab('profile'));
// Keeps the current content visible instead of flashing the fallback
```

Without the transition, switching tabs replaces working UI with a spinner, which feels worse than a brief delay.
